1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,200
[Lagos rush hour. A yellow danfo bus floats three metres above the jammed road, passengers squashed against the windows. Jonah dangles from the door by one glowing hand. A goat drifts past.]

2
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,708
PASSENGER: Driver! Why is the bus FLYING?!

3
00:00:03,058 --> 00:00:05,365
JONAH: Nobody panic! Everything is under control!

4
00:00:06,415 --> 00:00:09,565
[Wide: the bus rising toward a tangle of power lines. The whole street stares up, phones out.]

5
00:00:06,665 --> 00:00:10,896
EXECUTOR: This is Jonah Okafor. Yesterday, he fixed phones for a living.

6
00:00:11,246 --> 00:00:16,246
EXECUTOR: Today, he is holding a ten-tonne bus in the air. With one hand.

7
00:00:16,596 --> 00:00:22,750
EXECUTOR: And in about four seconds, he is going to make the biggest mistake of his life.

8
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:26,450
[Close on Jonah. Bus dust. His nose twitches. He sneezes — his glowing hand snaps shut — the bus lurches down — FREEZE FRAME.]

9
00:00:23,550 --> 00:00:26,977
JONAH: Please don't sneeze. Please don't sneeze. Please don't—

10
00:00:27,327 --> 00:00:29,181
JONAH: Ah... ah... CHOO!

11
00:00:30,731 --> 00:00:33,881
[Frozen mid-drop. The colour drains. THE BLINK FILES stamps across the frame.]

12
00:00:30,981 --> 00:00:35,562
EXECUTOR: What would you do... if everything you touched lost its gravity?

13
00:00:35,912 --> 00:00:44,758
EXECUTOR: To understand how a phone repair boy ended up here, we have to go back one day. To the night a star died.

14
00:00:45,908 --> 00:00:49,058
[One day earlier, golden hour. The camera glides down a loud, colourful Lagos street — okadas, vendors, a goat — and lands on a phone repair stall the size of a wardrobe.]

15
00:00:46,158 --> 00:00:50,388
EXECUTOR: Lagos. Twenty million people. And one very small phone repair stall.

16
00:00:51,338 --> 00:00:54,488
[Jonah holds up a phone so shattered it rattles. A customer waits, arms crossed.]

17
00:00:51,588 --> 00:00:55,400
JONAH: Madam... how many times did you drop this phone?

18
00:00:55,750 --> 00:00:56,650
CUSTOMER: Once.

19
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:58,538
JONAH: From where? An aeroplane?

20
00:00:59,488 --> 00:01:02,638
[Push in past Jonah to a faded rocket poster taped inside his stall: ONE DAY.]

21
00:00:59,738 --> 00:01:04,354
EXECUTOR: Jonah Okafor. Seventeen. He can fix anything with a wire in it.

22
00:01:04,704 --> 00:01:10,473
EXECUTOR: What he cannot fix is the feeling that his real life is happening somewhere else.

23
00:01:11,023 --> 00:01:14,173
[Tunde — sunglasses, gold bike — splashes through a puddle, soaking Jonah, and tosses him a cracked phone.]

24
00:01:11,273 --> 00:01:16,238
TUNDE: Oya, phone boy! Fix my screen. My daddy will pay you... eventually.

25
00:01:16,588 --> 00:01:19,281
JONAH: Your daddy has owed me since March.

26
00:01:19,831 --> 00:01:22,981
[Mrs. Bello arrives holding an old radio like it is made of glass. Her granddaughter Kemi, seven, holds her other hand.]

27
00:01:20,081 --> 00:01:23,927
MRS. BELLO: My husband's radio. It has been silent since he passed.

28
00:01:24,277 --> 00:01:26,200
JONAH: Give me ten minutes, Ma.

29
00:01:26,750 --> 00:01:29,900
[The radio crackles to life — music. Mrs. Bello offers money; Jonah pushes it back. Kemi solemnly hands him one toffee.]

30
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:32,769
EXECUTOR: He fixed it in nine. He would not take her money. So Kemi paid instead.

31
00:01:33,119 --> 00:01:36,581
KEMI: It's my last toffee. So it's worth a lot.

32
00:01:36,931 --> 00:01:40,392
JONAH: Then I'll save it for a very important day.

33
00:01:40,742 --> 00:01:43,435
EXECUTOR: Nobody else saw that. Remember it anyway.

34
00:01:43,985 --> 00:01:47,135
[Grandma Ada leans out of the house next door, pointing at an old fridge on the pavement.]

35
00:01:44,235 --> 00:01:47,696
GRANDMA: Jonah! Bring this fridge upstairs before I sell you!

36
00:01:48,046 --> 00:01:50,738
JONAH: Grandma, that fridge weighs more than me!

37
00:01:51,088 --> 00:01:52,242
GRANDMA: Then eat more!

38
00:01:53,092 --> 00:01:56,242
[Night. The camera rises up the house to the flat roof: water tanks, laundry lines, Lagos glittering. Jonah lies on a mat, staring up.]

39
00:01:53,342 --> 00:02:01,419
EXECUTOR: That night, Jonah did what he always did. He climbed to the roof, to look at places he would never go.

40
00:02:01,769 --> 00:02:02,669
JONAH: One day.

41
00:02:03,819 --> 00:02:06,969
[Tilt up into the stars. Orion draws itself; a red star on his shoulder pulses.]

42
00:02:04,069 --> 00:02:09,035
EXECUTOR: Up there is Orion. And on his shoulder... a red star. Betelgeuse.

43
00:02:09,985 --> 00:02:13,135
[Our Sun shrinks to a dot with the planets' orbits drawn around it. Betelgeuse swells out of the dot, swallowing Mercury, Venus, Earth, then Mars.]

44
00:02:10,235 --> 00:02:15,585
EXECUTOR: It is so big that if you put it where our Sun is...

45
00:02:15,935 --> 00:02:19,012
EXECUTOR: it would swallow Mercury. Venus. Earth. And Mars.

46
00:02:20,362 --> 00:02:23,512
[Betelgeuse flickers... and goes out. No explosion. Just a hole in Orion. Jonah sits up.]

47
00:02:20,612 --> 00:02:25,577
EXECUTOR: That night, it died. Not with an explosion. It just... switched off.

48
00:02:25,927 --> 00:02:28,585
JONAH: ...Did that star just turn off?

49
00:02:29,735 --> 00:02:32,885
[From the dark spot, twelve silver threads stream across space and fall onto Earth — one onto every continent. One curves toward West Africa.]

50
00:02:29,985 --> 00:02:33,831
EXECUTOR: But before it went dark, it sent something to Earth.

51
00:02:34,181 --> 00:02:35,081
EXECUTOR: Twelve somethings.

52
00:02:36,431 --> 00:02:39,581
[Jonah has fallen asleep on the roof. A silver thread drifts down and sinks into his open palm. It glows orange. He scratches his nose and snores.]

53
00:02:36,681 --> 00:02:42,415
EXECUTOR: One of them landed in the hand of a boy who was... fast asleep.

54
00:02:44,365 --> 00:02:47,515
[Pink dawn. Jonah wakes up three metres ABOVE the roof — upside down, blanket floating, Grandma's laundry orbiting him, the cat paddling the air.]

55
00:02:44,615 --> 00:02:47,658
JONAH: ...Why is the sky on the floor?

56
00:02:48,008 --> 00:02:50,700
EXECUTOR: The cat was not enjoying this either.

57
00:02:51,650 --> 00:02:54,800
[Jonah panics and clenches his fist — the glow dies — everything drops. Jonah splashes into the water drum. The laundry rains down onto Grandma in the yard below.]

58
00:02:51,900 --> 00:02:52,800
JONAH: AAAAH!

59
00:02:53,150 --> 00:02:56,612
GRANDMA: JONAH OKAFOR. Why is my underwear on my head?!

60
00:02:57,562 --> 00:03:00,712
[Morning. Dripping, Jonah crouches over a flip-flop. Open palm, glowing — touch — the flip-flop floats. Fist — it drops. Open — float. Fist — drop.]

61
00:02:57,812 --> 00:03:01,273
EXECUTOR: Jonah did what any scientist would do. He experimented.

62
00:03:01,623 --> 00:03:05,400
JONAH: Open hand... it floats. Close it... it falls.

63
00:03:05,950 --> 00:03:09,100
[Grandma climbs up, still wearing the laundry. Jonah hugs her — his hand does NOT glow. Her feet stay on the ground.]

64
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:10,012
JONAH: And when I don't want it to... nothing happens.

65
00:03:10,362 --> 00:03:13,823
GRANDMA: Why are you hugging me? What did you break?

66
00:03:14,373 --> 00:03:17,523
[A floating pearl prism with gold rings descends out of the morning sun. Jonah throws the flip-flop at it. It passes straight through.]

67
00:03:14,623 --> 00:03:16,162
EXECUTOR: Good morning, Jonah Okafor.

68
00:03:16,512 --> 00:03:23,050
EXECUTOR: That is the fourth flip-flop thrown at me today. I believe it is how humans say hello.

69
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:26,750
[The Executor projects a tiny hologram: the red star, and twelve threads falling. The twelfth lands in a tiny Jonah.]

70
00:03:23,850 --> 00:03:36,508
EXECUTOR: I am the last machine of the Vey — the people of the red star. Before their star went out, they gave away the one thing they could not take with them.

71
00:03:36,858 --> 00:03:42,592
EXECUTOR: Their gifts. Twelve of them. Yours is gravity. Or rather... the lack of it.

72
00:03:42,942 --> 00:03:44,865
JONAH: So I'm like an astronaut?

73
00:03:45,415 --> 00:03:48,565
[Hologram: Earth with the space station orbiting. A big arrow pulls the station toward Earth: 90%.]

74
00:03:45,665 --> 00:03:52,588
EXECUTOR: Funny thing about astronauts. Up there, gravity is still ninety percent as strong as it is down here.

75
00:03:52,938 --> 00:03:54,862
JONAH: Then why do they float?

76
00:03:55,412 --> 00:03:58,562
[Hologram: a cannon on a mountain. Shot one falls near. Shot two falls farther. Shot three falls all the way around the Earth and comes back.]

77
00:03:55,662 --> 00:04:02,969
EXECUTOR: Because they are falling. Fire a cannonball fast enough, and the ground curves away as fast as it falls.

78
00:04:03,319 --> 00:04:09,438
EXECUTOR: It falls around the Earth. Forever. Astronauts are not floating. They are falling... and missing.

79
00:04:09,788 --> 00:04:10,688
JONAH: And me?

80
00:04:11,038 --> 00:04:14,500
EXECUTOR: You, Jonah, do not need to fall at all.

81
00:04:15,050 --> 00:04:17,654
[The fridge on the pavement. Jonah taps it with a glowing finger; it lifts like a balloon. He nudges it up the stairs with one finger, grinning.]

82
00:04:15,300 --> 00:04:16,454
JONAH: Grandma! Watch this!

83
00:04:17,804 --> 00:04:20,954
[The fridge does not stop. It sails up the stairs and through Grandma's door. Hologram tags pop beside it: weight 0 kg — mass 80 kg.]

84
00:04:18,054 --> 00:04:24,977
EXECUTOR: Weightless is not the same as massless. The fridge weighed nothing. It still had eighty kilograms of stubbornness.

85
00:04:25,327 --> 00:04:26,227
GRANDMA: JONAH!

86
00:04:27,077 --> 00:04:30,227
[Bright noon. Tunde rides up mocking. Jonah taps the gold bike. It floats out from under Tunde and settles on top of a palm tree.]

87
00:04:27,327 --> 00:04:30,369
TUNDE: Phone boy! Is my screen ready, or—

88
00:04:30,719 --> 00:04:31,619
TUNDE: MY BIKE!

89
00:04:31,969 --> 00:04:36,585
EXECUTOR: Jonah had never been petty in his life. He decided to start.

90
00:04:37,135 --> 00:04:40,285
[Jonah touches his own chest. He rises — then hangs in mid-air above the market, swimming at nothing.]

91
00:04:37,385 --> 00:04:40,812
JONAH: Okay. Okay! Now... how do I go forward?

92
00:04:41,162 --> 00:04:46,931
EXECUTOR: Up there, you have nothing to push against. You will have to push something else.

93
00:04:47,481 --> 00:04:50,631
[He grabs oranges from Iya Osun's basket and hurls them backwards — each throw shoots him forwards over the market umbrellas. Gold arrows show push and push-back.]

94
00:04:47,731 --> 00:04:52,312
EXECUTOR: Every push comes with a push back. Throw something one way...

95
00:04:52,662 --> 00:04:55,319
JONAH: ...and I go the other way!

96
00:04:55,669 --> 00:04:57,592
IYA OSUN: My oranges! Who will pay?!

97
00:04:57,942 --> 00:05:00,250
JONAH: I'll pay you back, Iya! Tomorrow!

98
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:04,446
EXECUTOR: It is exactly how rockets fly. They throw fire backwards.

99
00:05:04,996 --> 00:05:08,146
[Everything he floated is slowly drifting upward: a chair, a flip-flop, the goat. Hologram arrows: air pushes up, like under a balloon. Jonah grabs the goat by the leg.]

100
00:05:05,246 --> 00:05:10,212
EXECUTOR: There was one small problem. Everything he made weightless... started to rise.

101
00:05:10,562 --> 00:05:18,254
EXECUTOR: Air pushes up on everything. Normally, weight wins. Take the weight away, and the air lifts it. Like a balloon.

102
00:05:18,604 --> 00:05:24,758
EXECUTOR: The goat would like it on record that it did not agree to any of this.

103
00:05:25,308 --> 00:05:28,458
[Three o'clock. Total gridlock. A danfo with a broken axle blocks the road. Behind it, an ambulance, lights spinning, going nowhere.]

104
00:05:25,558 --> 00:05:30,523
EXECUTOR: Three o'clock. Lagos traffic. One broken bus. And behind it... an ambulance.

105
00:05:30,873 --> 00:05:32,412
JONAH: I can move that.

106
00:05:32,762 --> 00:05:34,300
EXECUTOR: Jonah. Remember the balloon.

107
00:05:34,650 --> 00:05:36,188
JONAH: What could go wrong?

108
00:05:36,738 --> 00:05:39,888
[He slaps the bus. It lifts — and keeps lifting, passengers and all. He grabs the door and gets dragged up with it, toward the power lines. We are back where we started.]

109
00:05:36,988 --> 00:05:39,296
PASSENGER: Driver! Why is the bus FLYING?!

110
00:05:39,646 --> 00:05:42,338
JONAH: Okay, I've got it! I've got it!

111
00:05:42,688 --> 00:05:44,612
EXECUTOR: He did not have it.

112
00:05:45,162 --> 00:05:48,312
[The same sneeze. The fist snaps shut. The bus drops — passengers lift off their seats —]

113
00:05:45,412 --> 00:05:47,265
JONAH: Ah... ah... CHOO!

114
00:05:47,615 --> 00:05:55,273
EXECUTOR: And that brings us back to the mistake. Ten tonnes, three metres up, falling... and the ambulance right underneath.

115
00:05:56,423 --> 00:05:59,573
[Jonah's fingers half-open. The bus stops dropping and sinks like a feather. A ghostly hand diagram: open = float, half = gentle, closed = fall.]

116
00:05:56,673 --> 00:06:02,408
EXECUTOR: His hand had closed. And then, for the first time... he closed it slowly.

117
00:06:02,758 --> 00:06:04,962
JONAH: Slowly... slowly... slowly...

118
00:06:05,512 --> 00:06:08,662
[The bus kisses the road on the far side of the pothole. The ambulance roars through the gap. The passengers stumble out. The street erupts.]

119
00:06:05,762 --> 00:06:11,915
EXECUTOR: That is the most important rule of having a gift. Never let go all at once.

120
00:06:12,265 --> 00:06:15,692
PASSENGER: Young man... can you do my house next?

121
00:06:16,242 --> 00:06:19,392
[Phones everywhere. A view counter spins into the millions. Tunde, still bikeless, mutters.]

122
00:06:16,492 --> 00:06:18,381
TUNDE: ...Okay. That was cool.

123
00:06:18,731 --> 00:06:25,269
EXECUTOR: By sunset, eleven million people had watched it. One of them was the richest man on Earth.

124
00:06:25,819 --> 00:06:28,969
[A silver car far too wide for the street creeps in. The crowd parts. Desmond Kade steps out — immaculate, smiling.]

125
00:06:26,069 --> 00:06:28,762
KADE: Jonah Okafor. Desmond Kade. I build rockets.

126
00:06:29,112 --> 00:06:33,342
KADE: Do you know what the heaviest part of a rocket is?

127
00:06:33,892 --> 00:06:37,042
[A rocket in cut-away. Gold fuel fills it — almost the whole thing. A tiny capsule sits on top.]

128
00:06:34,142 --> 00:06:38,758
KADE: The fuel. Nine-tenths of every rocket is fuel, just to fight gravity.

129
00:06:39,108 --> 00:06:41,800
KADE: Now imagine a rocket that weighs nothing.

130
00:06:42,350 --> 00:06:45,500
[Kade hands Jonah a card. Behind Jonah, the ONE DAY rocket poster. Grandma stands in her doorway.]

131
00:06:42,600 --> 00:06:50,677
KADE: Float my rockets, and we go to Mars for the price of a bus ticket. My jet leaves tonight, at eight.

132
00:06:51,027 --> 00:06:51,927
JONAH: Mars...?

133
00:06:52,277 --> 00:06:56,508
GRANDMA: And who will fix the phones? Who will fix my fridge?

134
00:06:56,858 --> 00:06:58,012
JONAH: Grandma. It's MARS.

135
00:06:58,362 --> 00:07:02,942
GRANDMA: ...Then go. Before I cry in front of a rich man.

136
00:07:03,492 --> 00:07:06,642
[Sunset on the roof. Jonah packs a bag. The Executor projects a globe: twelve little lights scattered across the world.]

137
00:07:03,742 --> 00:07:11,435
EXECUTOR: Before you go. You are not the only one. Twelve gifts. Twelve people. Eleven of them do not know yet.

138
00:07:11,785 --> 00:07:12,938
JONAH: Does Kade know?

139
00:07:13,288 --> 00:07:14,442
EXECUTOR: He is counting.

140
00:07:14,992 --> 00:07:18,142
[7:40 PM. Rain hammers down. Jonah rides pillion on an okada toward the airport, bag on his back, Kade's message glowing on his phone: CAR WAITING · 8:00.]

141
00:07:15,242 --> 00:07:19,088
EXECUTOR: Seven-forty. Twenty minutes to his flight. The rain came early.

142
00:07:20,438 --> 00:07:23,588
[Behind them, a crack like thunder. The old market roof caves in on the stalls. Screams. Mrs. Bello stands in the rain.]

143
00:07:20,688 --> 00:07:22,996
MRS. BELLO: KEMI! My granddaughter is under there!

144
00:07:23,346 --> 00:07:24,850
JONAH: ...Stop the bike.

145
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:29,150
[Jonah slams his glowing palm onto the wreck. The whole roof lifts. People crawl out from under it — forty of them.]

146
00:07:26,250 --> 00:07:30,096
EXECUTOR: He made the whole roof weightless. Forty people crawled free.

147
00:07:31,246 --> 00:07:34,396
[But one slab keeps rising — Kemi is on it, clinging to a beam, drifting up into the storm. Lightning cracks above her.]

148
00:07:31,496 --> 00:07:36,846
EXECUTOR: But Kemi was still on it. And it was rising... into the lightning.

149
00:07:37,196 --> 00:07:38,096
KEMI: JONAH!

150
00:07:39,246 --> 00:07:42,396
[Jonah touches his chest and rises — far too slowly. The slab is getting away. He looks at the only thing in his hand: his phone, with Kade's message on it.]

151
00:07:39,496 --> 00:07:42,888
JONAH: Push something... I need to push something...

152
00:07:43,238 --> 00:07:46,700
EXECUTOR: His phone. His ticket. His way out of Lagos.

153
00:07:47,250 --> 00:07:50,400
[He hurls the phone straight down with everything he has — and rockets upward through the rain.]

154
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:49,038
EXECUTOR: He threw it away.

155
00:07:50,788 --> 00:07:53,938
[High above the market, Jonah catches Kemi. Lightning lights them both up. She holds on.]

156
00:07:51,038 --> 00:07:52,577
KEMI: Jonah? Are we flying?

157
00:07:52,927 --> 00:07:56,004
JONAH: Just for a little bit. Hold on tight.

158
00:07:56,554 --> 00:07:59,704
[His fist closes slowly. Jonah, Kemi and the whole roof sink gently through the rain and settle on the empty road. The street rushes in — Tunde too, pulling people from the stalls.]

159
00:07:56,804 --> 00:07:59,881
EXECUTOR: Slowly. Slowly. Never let go all at once.

160
00:08:00,231 --> 00:08:01,131
MRS. BELLO: Kemi!

161
00:08:02,281 --> 00:08:05,431
[Soaked, Jonah crouches and gives Kemi back her toffee. Tunde, dripping, stands awkwardly nearby.]

162
00:08:02,531 --> 00:08:03,685
KEMI: You kept it!

163
00:08:04,035 --> 00:08:07,881
JONAH: You said to save it for a very important day.

164
00:08:08,231 --> 00:08:10,888
TUNDE: ...I will pay for your phone.

165
00:08:11,438 --> 00:08:14,588
[Night. Jonah on the roof holding Grandma's ancient phone. Split screen: Kade in his jet cabin, the seat opposite him empty.]

166
00:08:11,688 --> 00:08:13,227
KADE: You missed your flight.

167
00:08:13,577 --> 00:08:14,731
JONAH: I was busy.

168
00:08:15,081 --> 00:08:22,773
KADE: So I saw. There are eleven more of you out there, Jonah. I wonder which of them won't be busy.

169
00:08:23,323 --> 00:08:26,473
[Inside. Grandma points at the battered fridge. Jonah floats it with one finger and sets it down — gently — exactly in its place.]

170
00:08:23,573 --> 00:08:25,112
GRANDMA: So. Mars is cancelled?

171
00:08:25,462 --> 00:08:26,615
JONAH: Mars can wait.

172
00:08:26,965 --> 00:08:28,504
GRANDMA: Good. The fridge cannot.

173
00:08:29,054 --> 00:08:32,204
[Across the street, Mrs. Bello's window glows. The radio plays. Kemi sleeps beside it, a toffee wrapper in her hand.]

174
00:08:29,304 --> 00:08:32,765
EXECUTOR: Jonah Okafor did not go to Mars that night.

175
00:08:33,115 --> 00:08:38,885
EXECUTOR: It turned out Lagos was not ready to let him go. And neither was he.

176
00:08:40,635 --> 00:08:43,785
[On the roof, Jonah looks at his palm. Twelve tiny lights glow there like a constellation. Then a thirteenth flickers on.]

177
00:08:40,885 --> 00:08:43,158
JONAH: Executor... why are there thirteen?

178
00:08:43,508 --> 00:08:45,046
EXECUTOR: Twelve gifts were sent.

179
00:08:45,896 --> 00:08:46,796
EXECUTOR: Thirteen arrived.

180
00:08:47,146 --> 00:08:49,804
EXECUTOR: That... is not supposed to happen.

181
00:08:51,154 --> 00:08:54,304
[Yorkshire, rain. A 71-year-old woman in a purple cardigan leaps clean over a red double-decker bus, handbag and all, and lands in a hedge.]

182
00:08:51,404 --> 00:08:56,019
EXECUTOR: Next: a seventy-one-year-old grandmother in Yorkshire who just jumped over a bus.

183
00:08:56,369 --> 00:08:57,908
MAGS: Well. That is new.

184
00:08:59,058 --> 00:09:02,208
[The Executor floats in the dark among the stars.]

185
00:08:59,308 --> 00:09:03,119
EXECUTOR: And if you're wondering whether it could be you...

186
00:09:04,069 --> 00:09:05,223
EXECUTOR: I'm still reading.
